Hi Guys,

First of all, thanks for the awesome tutorials online, and the slides to download, really helped me through the DSM process I've been following the helpful tips here for a while.

I've been creating DSM for 7 species of ungulate in a Savannah ecosystem, based on an extensive transect data set spanning several years. I've split this into seasonal classifications and I'm looking at the spatial abundance changes across three seasons (wet, dry, and drought). I've used a spatial smooth term s(x,y) for all models, and have investigated the effect of four variables (distance to water, distance to settlement, NDVI, NDVI - Seasonal variation)  Just a few questions:
For some species in some seasons I have a very severe gradient across the study area often caused by management. For example, cattle in certain areas. This is mostly fine, but the fitted surface has increased unrealistically as predictions are made further from the survey effort. After reading around I have used Duchon splines over Thin plate splines for the s(x,y) term. This helped for most problematic sets, but not all, any thoughts on this?
 I've split the data into certain seasonal classifications and multiplied the amount of effort accordingly, but are there any other ways to look at the temporal trends. The surveys occurred monthly for nearly two years, and all variable data is available at a monthly scale. I saw briefly in some of the slides about tensors for space-time?
I saw last weeks message about point counts, I'd be very interested in looking at how DSM can be applied to point counts, which we have a good database of collection for ungulates.
Thanks again in advance,

